WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.530
Before we start this video which is part of
the greater Project Pannonia, I want to stop

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.280
anyone angrily typing in the comments about
me not using the word Magyar in the video.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.130
This is because even though yes when they
came in to Europe they called themselves the

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:23.320
Magyar tribes all early written sources about
the Hungarians call them “Ungry” or “Ungary”.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.960
And it is this latin name that today’s English
name of Hungary comes from.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.920
And since I am going to be dealing with only
early Hungarian history it made sense to call

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.330
the people the name they use in the sources,
it also makes it more simple to understand

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.650
as I wont be switching between two different
names.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.750
I hope you understand.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:48.710
As I have mentioned many times in my other
videos on the early Slavic History, the 9th

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.310
to 10th century Hungarian arrival in to central
Europe shifted many power dynamics around

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:58.060
the Pannonian plain and pretty much paved
the way for the creation of the medieval european

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.640
borders we are familiar with today.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.211
However at the time the arrival of nomadic
conquering people in to the Pannonian plain

00:01:04.211 --> 00:01:07.830
was just a newsworthy as the Franks fighting
another civil war.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:12.990
It was nothing new and nothing unexpected,
many conquering tribes have come to the pannonian

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.329
plain before the Hungarians, and many will
most likely come after them.

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:21.479
But to the surprise of many, this did not
happen, Hungarians where the last nomadic

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:24.799
tribe to come and settle in to the Pannonian
basin.

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:28.909
But how exactly did the Hungarians come in
to the Pannonian plane and what exactly did

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:31.789
they do before then, isn’t fully known.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:36.890
We have some contemporary writings about them
but these only present a very fragmented timeline

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:41.080
that doesn’t even mention any events before
their arrival in to central Europe.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.030
We also have some writings by the Hungarians
themselves.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.780
But these were mostly written two to 3 hundred
years later, well after they were settled

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.259
and christianized.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:54.929
Nonetheless combining all these sources together
along with archeology and linguistic can give

00:01:54.929 --> 00:02:00.670
us a still fragmented but more detailed picture
of the early history of the Hungarians.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.179
Today it is mostly agreed, although not without
its opponents, that the Hungarian language

00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:10.260
and people come from the larger Ugro-Finnic
group but by this point far removed.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.390
Far removed because it is believed that the
Hungarians where one of the earliest ugro-finnic

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:19.410
tribes to venture out of the Ural mountains
in to the Eastern European plain around 800

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.410
BCE.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:25.340
Making them isolated for much of their history
and significantly influenced in language and

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.550
culture by various other people groups they
came in to contact with.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:34.780
Where exactly the Hungarians where and went
during this time starting in around 800 BCE

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.750
to 800 CE is highly debated.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.470
But just to give you an idea one of the theories
is as follows.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.860
The Hunagrians established themselves in the
vicinity of the Oka, Don and Volga Rivers

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.920
in around 800 BCE.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.940
It was here they came in to contact with Iranian
groups like Scythians and Sarmatians and also

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.360
adopted the nomadic steppe lifestyle.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.630
This would also explain the Iranian lone words
in the Hungairan language and maybe even some

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.770
borrowed Iranian folk tales.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:06.290
Then after the Hunnic migration through the
area around the 3rd and 4th centuries we know

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:11.220
that Iranian groups got mostly replaced by
Turkic groups in the Europen steppes.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.190
Such as the Avars, Sabirs, Khazars, Bulgars,
etc.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:19.150
It was here that the Hungarians may have gained
some of their turkic cultural influences and

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.250
borrowed some of the turkic words that are
in their language.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.511
Then after the fall of the Huns followed by
the subsequent migrations of the Avars, Slavs

00:03:26.511 --> 00:03:32.230
and Bulgars in the 7th century the Hungarians
were forced south in to the now Khazar Khaganate.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:36.470
Here they had a complicated relationship with
the Khazars probably switching between being

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:39.310
independent, allies, vassals and so on.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:43.280
However while in contact with the Khazars
the Hungarians borrowed even more turkic words

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.080
and assimilated various new people groups
in to their tribe.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.300
Then due to the push by the Varangians and
slavs from the north and the Khazars and Pechenegs

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.540
from the East and South the Hunagrians slowly
moved to the area known as Etelkoz by the

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.060
8th to 9th century.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.440
Now this is only one of many theories to explain
what happened to the Hungarian people during

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.070
800 BCE to around 800 CE.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:08.830
There’s other theories claiming that the
Hungarians migrated from the East of the Ural

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:12.760
mountains not west, there’s some claiming
that Hunagrians first came out of the Ural

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.829
mountains south settling near the Ural river
and slowly being pushed west, and even some

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:21.900
saying they settled all the way south near
the Caucuses, etc.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.229
We may probably never know for sure what happened
to the Hungarians during this time but the

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:29.210
facts we know for sure are as follows.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:32.439
Hungarians early on diverged from the larger
Finno Ugric group around the Ural mountains.

00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:37.670
They adopted the nomadic or semi nomadic steppe
lifestyle and had at some point Iranian, Slavic

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.770
but mostly Turkic influences in their language
and culture.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.379
They were also somehow intertwined at some
point with the politics of the Khazar Khaganate.

00:04:45.379 --> 00:04:49.271
Lastly we know that when they show up in the
European written records in the 9th century

00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:54.620
coming from the area known as Etelkoz today’s
South Ukraine, they were very much a polyethnic

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.680
group that wasn’t a rigidly defined close
nite ethnic society, but a group of tribes

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.300
of varying degrees of ethnic similarity joined
together by a common goal and or alliance

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:06.300
and or a leader.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.050
This doesn’t mean they were all completely
different tribes that didn’t even speak

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:13.360
the same language, it just means that the
Hungarians that came in to central Europe

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.889
weren’t a completely homogenous group of
people as used to be believed before.

00:05:17.889 --> 00:05:22.110
Anything else about the Hungarians outside
of what I just listed is very much guess work

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.539
at this point.

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:31.210
The first certain written record about the
Hunagrians is from 836 or 37 in the Chronicle

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.889
by George the Monk.

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:37.889
It states that the Hungarian warriors from
Etelkoz fought alongside the Bulgarian forces

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:42.419
against the Byzantines, most likely being
paid by the Bulgarians to do so.

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:46.430
This plausibly wasn’t the first Hunagrian
involvement in Europe but it was the first

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:52.529
none disputed recorded one, and it also perfectly
sets precedents for the Hungarians as mercenaries

00:05:52.529 --> 00:05:56.990
and raiders, since that’s what they mostly
did for the next 100 plus years.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:01.520
The first mention of Hungarians in Western
European records comes from Frankish annals

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:08.840
which report a devastating Hungarian raid
in east Frankia during the winter of 862-63.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.460
This raid occurred during the rebellion of
Carloman who if you remember from my western

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.740
slavic video was supported by the moravian
king Rastislav.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.880
It is believed that the Hungarians came due
to Carloman’s but mainly Rastislava’s

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.210
request to help aid in the rebellion.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:26.110
This is further supported by the fact that
in a 9th century letter from the Salzburg

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.800
bishoprick, we learn that the Hunagrians were
oftentimes fighting on the side of the Moravians,

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.210
so Rastislav’s intisment of the Hunagrians
to come help him and Carloman during this

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.399
time, wouldn’t be that far fetched.

00:06:37.399 --> 00:06:41.360
… Ha, Hungarians and Moravians fighting
together, who would have thought ….

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.360
Anyways, the Hungarians came back in 881 again
on the side of the Moravians fighting against

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:48.389
the Franks.

00:06:48.389 --> 00:06:53.380
After this event we don’t have any accounts
mentioning the Hungarians up until 892, however

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:57.599
it is almost certain that during this time
they orchestrated numerous frequent raids

00:06:57.599 --> 00:07:01.839
in to their neighboring realms and ended their
long standing alliance with the Moravians

00:07:01.839 --> 00:07:03.439
for reasons we don’t know.

00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:09.379
Then in 892 to 894 Hungarians allied themselves
with the Franks and helped the Frankish king

00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:13.750
Arnulf to basically cripple Moravia at the
height of its power.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:18.040
This two sided attack along with the death
of Svetopluk destroyed any Moravian control

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:22.749
in the Pannonian basin and through the entire
kingdom in to disarray with Svatopluks sons

00:07:22.749 --> 00:07:25.279
fighting amongst each other over control.

00:07:25.279 --> 00:07:30.430
This meant that even though Western Panonia
was taken by the Franks, Eastern Panonia had

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:31.430
a powervacume.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:35.499
It is plausible that during this time the
Hungarians established frontier military outpost

00:07:35.499 --> 00:07:39.620
in eastern Panonia, to cement their control
in the newly conquered territory but this

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:40.629
is unclear.

00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:46.029
Nonetheless in 895 the Hungarians turned their
attention to the Bulgars who arrived at the

00:07:46.029 --> 00:07:48.120
very gates of Constantinople.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:53.240
The Byzantene Emperor Leo the VI asked the
Hungarians for help, who took this opportunity

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:55.770
and started raiding the Bulgarian kingdom.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:08:00.439
Due to this they managed to destroy any kind
of remaining Bulgarian control in Transylvania

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:04.349
finally opening up that entire area for a
plausible settlement.

00:08:04.349 --> 00:08:08.659
The Bulgarians had no choice but to retreat
from Constantinople and face the Hungarian

00:08:08.659 --> 00:08:09.819
army head on.

00:08:09.819 --> 00:08:15.080
The Hungarians saved Constantoniple, and to
thanked them, Leo VI made a quick peace treaty

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:18.080
with the Bulgars and left the Hungarians out
to dry.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:23.599
The Hungarians where now stuck in an unfavorable
war and to add to all this the Bulgars sent

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:28.550
envoys to the Pechenegs, asking them to destroy
the Hungarian “homeland” while they deal

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.820
with the main army, it was during this time
that some of the Hungarians being attacked

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.070
by the Pechengs started to migrate across
the Carpathians.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:41.959
The following year in 896 the Hungarians were
defeated by the Bulgars and retreated back

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:46.899
home, where they found that “all their women
and children where either killed or taken

00:08:46.899 --> 00:08:48.660
away by the Pechenegs”.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.230
The author of the Annales Fuldenses continues
to say that after this “they decided to

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:59.170
seek greener pastures, as well as women to
bear their children, west of the Carpathians”.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.920
More specifically the lands they have just
helped release from the Moravian and Bulgarian

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:03.920
control.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.029
Whether the Hungarians already controlled
the area through aforementioned military outposts

00:09:08.029 --> 00:09:14.769
since around 892 or just returned to the area
with the intention of reconquering it in 895-6

00:09:14.769 --> 00:09:16.279
is unknown.

00:09:16.279 --> 00:09:20.520
Nonetheless, even if that were true, the main
Hungarian settlement in Eastern Pannonia and

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.320
Transilvania still started in around 895 and
continued pretty much until 900.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.279
In 898 Arnulf still having his alliance with
the Hungarians used them to raid in to the

00:09:32.279 --> 00:09:37.200
Northern Italina lands as he had lots of trouble
subduing that area under his control.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:42.779
However as the Hungarians returned to Pannonia
in 900 they found out that Arnulf died, meaning

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:45.029
their alliance with the Franks ended.

00:09:45.029 --> 00:09:49.250
With this news, when they resumed their frequent
raids on the neighboring realms they also

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:51.079
ventured in to the Frankish Kingdom.

00:09:51.079 --> 00:09:56.100
Due to this in 901, Franks and Moravians,
bitter rivals for almost a 100 years signed

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.930
a peace treaty and started a defensive cooperate
against the Hungarian incursions.

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:03.709
The events of the next 5 years are uncertain.

00:10:03.709 --> 00:10:07.910
We can gather from short mentions in some
annals that the Moravian, Frankish alliance

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:12.970
manage to defend itself until about 903 but
then it's seemed like the Hungarians started

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.300
to gain an upper hand.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:18.880
In the following year the Hungarians raided
again in to Italy this time helping the Italian

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.520
principalities against the Frankish king,
and they also managed to annex at some point

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.620
the Frankish controlled western Pannonia.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.440
Then in 906 they took control of the remaining
Moravian lands, defeated the Bohemians and

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:32.870
raided all the way north in to Saxony.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:36.800
They also created many new settlements in
these newly conquered territories moving the

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.870
center of the Hunagrian Population more westward.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:45.339
By 907 the Hungarian control stretched from
the rivers of Morava and Leitha all the way

00:10:45.339 --> 00:10:47.500
down to Sava, and the Danube.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:52.880
They basically controlled the entire Pannonian
plain but this control was fragile at best.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.630
They needed a clear and decisive victory against
the franks to solidify these newly conquered

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:58.630
holdings.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:01.750
And so the stage was set for the battle of
Pressburg.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:05.069
However before continuing I want to address
the fact why I haven’t talked much about

00:11:05.069 --> 00:11:07.600
internal Hungairan affairs during this video.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:12.000
This is because all the contemporary sources
that exist from that time don’t talk about

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.000
this.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.779
The only two sources that do talk about this,
are the Gesta Hungarorum and Chronicon Pictum,

00:11:17.779 --> 00:11:20.769
written in the 13th and 14th century respectively.

00:11:20.769 --> 00:11:24.920
Way after the 9th and 10th centuries conquests
of the Hungarians.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:29.671
Plus a lot of their accounts of early hungarian
history is purely fictional like Almos the

00:11:29.671 --> 00:11:34.980
father Arpad being related to Atila the Hun,
or the whole story of the Blood Oath.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:40.320
To add to all this, these two sources often
times contradict each other, so at best one

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.910
of them has to be made up.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:46.110
Due to all this Historians reluctantly use
these sources alone when dealing with the

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:51.310
early Hunagrian history and try to combine
them wherever possible with outside writings,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.690
hence the historical view is mostly lacking
in Hunagrian internal affairs.

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:59.579
With that said there are things we can tell
with pretty good certainty thanks to this

00:11:59.579 --> 00:12:00.579
approach.

00:12:00.579 --> 00:12:04.819
For example we know that the Hungarians had
a dual monarchy system with one ruler being

00:12:04.819 --> 00:12:07.389
a religious figure and the other a military
one.

00:12:07.389 --> 00:12:12.459
We also know that these rulers during the
Hunagrian Pannonia conquest where Arpad (religious)

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:13.820
and Kurszán (military).

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:18.480
However in 904 when the Hungarians started
to gain the upper hand in the Pannonian conquest,

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:23.940
the Frankish king Louis the 3rd asked Kurszán
to come to a Frankish banquet for peace negotiations.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:28.959
Kurszan obliged but when he showed up the
Franks quickly killed him along with his entourage.

00:12:28.959 --> 00:12:33.470
The Frankish nobles were hoping this would
cause chaos within the Hungarian tribes as

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:38.569
their ruler was now dead, but since there
was still Arpad, he just assumed both of the

00:12:38.569 --> 00:12:42.329
kings roles, ending the Hungarian system of
dual monarchs.

00:12:42.329 --> 00:12:46.029
And since Arpad was technically the first
“King” (singilur) of the Hunagrians, he’s

00:12:46.029 --> 00:12:49.879
the one celebrated as the leader who brought
his people to Pannonia.

00:12:49.879 --> 00:12:54.600
The date of Arpads death along with who was
his successor is uncertain but we do know

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.830
for sure he didn’t live past 907.

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.420
Which brings us back to the battle of Pressburg,
today’s Bratislava.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.750
Renamed to Johannes Aventinus

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:09.089
Even though this battle is one of the most
important Battles in Hungarian history it

00:13:09.089 --> 00:13:11.779
is surrounded with lots of uncertainty.

00:13:11.779 --> 00:13:16.540
For example the only complete source we have
on the battle comes from a 16th century writer

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:18.199
Johannes Turmair.

00:13:18.199 --> 00:13:22.199
He claims to be working from primary Frankish
sources that have been since lost to time

00:13:22.199 --> 00:13:25.519
but that hasn’t stopped some Historians
doubting his writings.

00:13:25.519 --> 00:13:29.050
With that said I am going to be presenting
his version of the battle in this video but

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:33.440
before then here’s the list of all the things
we know for sure that happened if Turmair’s

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.440
entire writings are disregarded.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:35.440
The battle took place at some point in 907
most likely in Juli.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:36.440
Luitpold, Margrave of Bavaria died along with
much of the Bavarian nobility.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:37.440
Archbishop Theotmar of Salzburg along with
2 other Bishops died.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:38.440
Hungarians scored a decisive victory.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:39.440
Yeah, that’s it.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:40.959
Historians can’t even agree on the exact
location of the battle if Turmair is disregarded.

00:13:40.959 --> 00:13:46.130
With that said the reason for the Frankish
army entering Hungarian lands in 907 was three

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:47.529
fold.

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:52.420
Defeat the Hungarian army so they will stop
their raiding expeditions in to Francia.

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:55.350
Reconquer the lost Frankish lands of Pannonia.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.959
Free Moravia and incorporate in to the Frankish
Kingdom.

00:13:58.959 --> 00:14:03.130
Basically the Franks looked at this expedition
as a big hail mary to destroy the Hungarians

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:07.120
just as Charlamagne did with the Avars in
the 8th and 9th century.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:10.880
At the head of this Frankish army was the
Bavarian duke Luitpold who continued this

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:15.709
idea of walking in the footsteps of Charlemagne
by entering the Hungarian territory the same

00:14:15.709 --> 00:14:17.100
way Charlamagne did.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:21.269
He divided his army in to three smaller armies,
one marching on the north side of the danube

00:14:21.269 --> 00:14:25.320
of which he took command the other on the
south side of the danube and the last one

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:30.379
sailing on the danube acting as a communication
and supply bridge between the two.

00:14:30.379 --> 00:14:34.790
However, problems started right away as the
Hunagrians employed a nomadic hit and run

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:38.329
method, constantly harassing both sides of
the Frankish army.

00:14:38.329 --> 00:14:42.250
This resulted in a large confusion among the
divided armies and due to this confusion the

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:46.959
army on the south bank, managed to march way
ahead of the two other armies.

00:14:46.959 --> 00:14:51.180
They decided to stop so the rest of the army
could catch up but that’s when the Hungairans

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.300
attacked in full force.

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:57.590
Destroying the entire unprepared south bank
army without the rest of the army not even

00:14:57.590 --> 00:14:58.850
knowing about it.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:06.320
When night fell Luitpold decided to make camp
somewhere near Pressburg, thinking he will

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:09.269
catch up to the south bank army tomorrow.

00:15:09.269 --> 00:15:13.100
However the Hunagrians under the cover of
night crossed the danube and attacked the

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:15.129
unsuspecting Frankish camp.

00:15:15.129 --> 00:15:18.310
Killing most of the soldiers along with Luitpold
himself.

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:22.330
The next day the Hunagrians destroyed the
danubian fleet by setting it on fire with

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:23.649
fire arrows.

00:15:23.649 --> 00:15:26.519
And so the Battle of Pressburg was over.

00:15:26.519 --> 00:15:31.149
Hungarians had their decisive victory and
solidified their control over the entire Pannonian

00:15:31.149 --> 00:15:33.440
Plain along with all the people living in
it.

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:38.350
This battle made it clear to everyone that
the Hungarians were here to stay and so the

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:46.100
next chapter of the Hungarian History began,
the Central European chapter.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:49.680
